  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Justice and peace programme</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To develop &apos;economic justice&apos; as a central theme in the current national church discourse.
To build a common analysis and commitment to action on socio-economic rights within the whole Justice &amp; Peace network.
To contribute to the development of a common, &apos;economic justice&apos; informed social policy perspective among all national Catholic agencies.
To increase the capacity of targetted local groups to engage in economic justice advocacy by developing a competent level of information, analytical and organisational skill and commitment among a defined group of local church-based economic justice activists.
To support and strengthen the ecumenical economic justice movement through the initiation of and participation in joint events, publications, and through common analysis and reflection towards a common perspective and platform.
To ensure the presence and participation of the South African church perspective at key international (regional and global) events relevant to economic justice, so as to contribute to the building of a strong international economic justice movement and to a stronger advocacy profile for Southern Africa civil society structures.
To parallel the South African government&apos;s socio-economic delivery programmes from an appropriately-informed economic justice perspective.
To engage in research and analysis on key national and international socio-economic development policy frameworks so as to recommend appropriate policy and advocacy positions to the SACBC.
To generate information and resource materials for natioal advocacy in favour of economic justice.
To promote and provide support to ongoing civil society economic justice platforms, campaigns, and movements (eg. The People&apos;s Budget campaign, the apartheid debt and reparations campaign, Jubilee South Africa, the National Poverty Forum, the campaign to make banks accessible to the poor, and the Treatment Action Campaign.</narrative>
  </description>
